  ---

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-task-notifications-design.md[39m (not committed — I added
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers[39m to [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m).

  [1mWhere we landed, versus where we started.[0m You asked for a notifications system. The repo turned out to be an 11-line
  HTML file, so what we actually specced is a single-user task app whose reason for existing is its notification panel.
  The four subsystems the original request implied collapsed to one small one, because single-user removed identity and
  interest entirely, and time-based triggers removed the need for change tracking.

  Three things got cut along the way that are worth remembering as cuts, not oversights:

  - [1mRecurring tasks and per-task reminders[0m — the two most expensive fields, neither selected
  - [1mOS-level notifications[0m — a permission prompt for no gain over a panel you're looking at
  - [1mStored notification records[0m — derived + snooze gets dismissal without the dedup and invalidation bugs

  And one constraint that will shape expectations: [1mnothing fires while the tab is closed.[0m There's no server and no
  service worker. If you ever want a 9am "you have three overdue tasks" push, that requires a backend, and it's the one
  thing this architecture can't grow into cheaply.

  Please review the spec and tell me if you want changes. Once you approve it, the next step is the writing-plans skill
  to turn it into an implementation plan — I won't write any code before then.

